//
// Normative rule sstatus_spp (src/priv/supervisor.adoc):
//   The SPP bit indicates the privilege level at which a hart was executing before entering
//     supervisor mode. When a trap is taken, SPP is set to 0 if the trap originated from user mode,
//     or 1 otherwise. When an SRET instruction is executed to return from the trap handler, the
//     privilege level is set to user mode if the SPP bit is 0, or supervisor mode if the SPP bit is
//     1; SPP is then set to 0.
//
// Context:
//   EBREAK traps are delegated to S-mode (medeleg[3]), so they are taken into S from S and U. The
//     trap log records sstatus at handler entry; the window checks SPP through it (the mode the
//     trap came from).
//
// Test plan:
//   Before each trap SPP is preset to the opposite of its expected value.
//   1. From S-mode with SPP=0: EBREAK traps with SPP=1 (also with SPP preset 1).
//   2. From U-mode entered by SRET (which leaves SPP=0) and from U-mode entered through T-SBI with
//     SPP preset to 1: EBREAK traps with SPP=0.
//   3. SRET with SPP=1 lands at sepc in S-mode: a read of sstatus does not trap there, and SPP
//     reads 0.
//   4. SRET with SPP=0 lands at sepc in U-mode: the same read of sstatus raises illegal-instruction
//     from U.
//
// Approximate dynamic instruction count: 1081
//

